      ],
      "text": "scientists will be studying coral species,' said Pearce-Kelly. One aim will be to\ncarry out research on species to find those that can survive best in warm,\nacidic waters. Another will be to try to increase coral breeding rates. 'Coral\nspawn just once a year,' he added. 'However, aquarium-based research has\nenabled some corals to spawn artificially, which can assist coral reef\nrestoration efforts. And if this can be extended for all species, we could\nconsider the launching of coral-spawning programmes several times a year.\nThat would be a big help in restoring blighted reefs.'\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        255.6920623779297,
        82.23104858398438,
        275.1934509277344
      ],
      "text": "F.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        275.793701171875,
        522.5350952148438,
        433.6736145019531
      ],
      "text": "Research in these fields is being conducted in laboratories around the world,\nwith the London Zoo centre linked to this global network. Studies carried out in\none centre can then be tested in others. The resulting young coral can then be\ndisplayed in the tank in Tiny Giants. 'The crucial point is that the progress we\nmake in making coral better able to survive in a warming world can be shown\nto the public and encourage them to believe that we can do something to save\nthe planet's reefs,' said Pearce-Kelly. 'Saving our coral reefs is now a critically\nimportant ecological goal.'\n"
    },
